A||||11||87FieroGTx||Max power from a 4.9?||||1||
Z||000000||87FieroGTx||12-20-2001||08:30 PM|||| I was talking to a friend tonight and he said I should look into the 4.9. I guess his father had one putting out 350hp and would rev to 6,000.<P> So what's the max N/A HP you can get from a 4.9?<P> Being aluminum Can you bore/stroke them?<P> Will a turbo/SC work?<P> How much does it cost to put a stock 4.9 into a fiero?<P><BR> I'm still leaning toward the 3.4tdc/N*/Aurora but who knows.||||reg||1
Z||000001||rockcrawl||12-20-2001||09:51 PM||||200hp, 275 ft-lb stock. If that's not enough, you should probably get a northstar or SBC.||||reg||1
Z||000002||cadero2dmax||12-20-2001||10:08 PM||||I am interested in what this guy did to get those kind of numbers from a 300 cubic inch Caddy engine. The intake manifold and intake runners are extremely limited in diameter, as are the exhaust ports and manifolds. So limited that I doubt that this kind of horsepower could be realized without putting several more thousand in it than would be worthwhile. <P>If one were to open the breathing up, I think he would be decreasing that "git-it-on" torque that is there the instant you get off idle, pulling equally strong to the limited RPM redline.<P>Also the valvetrain is so weak that it would<BR>take a bundle to get the RPM to go that high.<BR>As Rockcrawl said, look elsewhere if that is what you want. It would cost more to get there than it is worth.<P>To answer your questions, this is probably the easiest conversion except for the 3.4 that there is. It is far easier than the N*, 3.8, or SBC. There is nothing on the car to cut, you simply put the appropriate motor mounts on, change to a smaller starter (or cut a little off the starter flange), change to a smaller alternator (the stock Fiero will do), and trim the right trunk hinge just a tad. Then there is the matter of plumbing the coolant lines, fuel lines, and exhaust. Install an oil cooler, and then put the ECM in and start wiring.<P>The engine is sleeved, can accept a bore.<P>I would think that the bottom end is strong enough to accept a boost from a blower, either turbo or supercharger. But I don't know what would be accomplished. The top end breathing is not good enough that the extra charge could find it's way in there efficiently.<P>The advantages are that it is probably the cheapest swap this side of a quad 4. It has oh-my-god, slam-you-in-the-back-of-your-seat and break-the-tires-loose-at-will torque. Plus, it is the cheapest low 13's swap you will find that still can give 26mpg and a long life. <P>Most 100,000 mile 4.9 motors still have some of the crosshatch pattern still visible on the cylinder walls, the clearances must be tremendous (also why low horsepower, high torque at low RPM with a limited redline - - sorta like a diesel - right?). This engine is built to go a long time - sorta like a diesel, huh?<P>Could you introduce me to the automotive engineering genius that can work the kind of magic you mentioned above with his 4.9 engine? I work for an airline, so it wouldn't cost me anything to visit and get his "secrets", since there is absolutely no speed parts available on the market for this engine. No headers, no chip, no hot ignition components. No cams, no performance pistons, well - -you get the idea!!<P>You either like what the 4.9 has to offer out of the box in it's basic stock configuration, or you call Archie. It is that simple.<P>G<p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-20-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000003||artherd||12-21-2001||06:09 AM||||The 4.9 has a 4800rpm redline....<P>It's not my bag, but manages to be pretty fast anyway.<P>George said it best, either 200hp at 4800 (and 275-lb-ft at? 2000-4000?  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"> makes you get a big goofy grin, or you'd better do a 7-grand DOHC motor swap.<P>They're VERY different beasts!<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>PS: The caddy v8 is hands down, the cheapest V8.  For the price of a 3.4, it walks all over that sad v6)<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||1
