Z||000007||87FieroGTx||12-21-2001||01:09 PM|||| When I asked him for the specs he said it was a drag engine with no exhaust a custom hi-rise sheet metal intake and SBC carb but that's all he knew <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/frown.gif"> He said he would ask his father next time they talked.<P> Personally I think he was full of BS "As a lot of my friends tend to be <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">" but I'll ask him if I can give out his email so you can get in touch with him.<P>I think 350hp would be doable with the right parts but for the $$ you could have went SBC anyway.||||reg||1
Z||000008||Mach10||12-21-2001||01:27 PM||||It's not impossible, or even that expensive... The point is that it'd be FAR cheaper to coax that kind of power out of a SBC... <BR>Power = Time x Money<BR>Simple as that. The caddy starts cheap, but the utter lack of aftermarket makes Money increase exponentially. I don't think 250HP is unreasonable with a $4000 rebuild... But consider that the same power from an SBC will run you 1100...  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><BR>I'm still going for it. The little-to-no weight gain is very appealing... I don't think I'd need that much more.||||reg||6
Z||000009||cadero2dmax||12-21-2001||07:14 PM||||I think the Caddy has the POTENTIAL to get those numbers (350hp, etc). But I don't think the performance would improve much, and in a lot of arenas would provide poorer performance. Here's why - -<P>After my experience de-tuning the racing Quad four engine to get enough torque to make the car go well, I learned that hp isn't s#$t. I originally had 256hp on the dyno at Mantapart when the engine was built. That engine was crankin' horsepower numbers that made my chest swell up - 256hp out of a normally aspirated 2.3 liters - - now THAT's impressive!! The part that I didn't tell is that the peak horsepower was around 5400RPM,  with only around 180lbft of peak torque at around 4800RPM. Big numbers, terrible performance.<P>I wanted mid range torque, I planned on autocrossing and doing Pro Solo with the car, with a jaunt down the 1/4 once in a while.<P>We detuned the motor - milder cams, more restrictive exhaust, smaller TB, and re-curved ECM. It ended up 238hp (still more than a hundred ponies per liter!) with 208lbft of torque. Both the horsepower and torque came on at a lower rpm, and had a flatter curve. The Fiero performed (and actually is still winning races) a lot better than when it had the "potent" 250+ horses.<P>Now, take that concept to the Caddy, and reverse it. The 4.x already has the restricted intake and exhaust, mild cam and mild ECM. Trying to change any or all of that would give bigger horsepower, but I fear that it would lose the torque that makes this motor so great, and move the power curve higher in the RPM range. Trust me, that would decrease performance, the power wouldn't be there at will all the way up the limited RPM range the way it is stock.<P>BTW, for a drag car, 350 hp is not much. My sons daily driver Camaro makes a lot more than that, and I'll bet a cup of coffee that he hasn't thrown nearly as much $ at his car to get there, either. He is in the low 12's in Denver (Rocky Mountain High - no air to put in the motor at Bandemere. Probably get into the 11's here in 600' altitude Tulsa).<P>I am wondering what this wild Caddy really does in the 1/4 - mid 12's? That's good, a little better than stock, but a good running Archies LS1 gets that and more easily!!<P>As I have stated over and over, find a Cadero, drive a Cadero, then come back and tell us what it is all about. The numbers do not do this car right. Where I drive (light to light, autocross - you know, REAL WORLD) the Caddy probably does better than most of the higher dollar, higher number cars. For the longer races, the full quarter, etc. - - well, it runs out of breath. But those that beat me aren't getting 26mpg, either!!<P>------------------<BR>George Ryan<BR>A pair of 4.9 Cadero's<P>For Racing Photos of my former Q4 Fiero racecar, go to: <BR>http://albums.photopoint.com/j/AlbumIndex?u=142520&a=1342821 <P>(until I can figure out how to post photos)||||reg||1
Z||000010||Will||12-21-2001||07:46 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by cadero2dmax:<BR><B>The part that I didn't tell is that the peak horsepower was around 5400RPM,  with only around 180lbft of peak torque at around 4800RPM. Big numbers, terrible performance.<BR></B><P>Something does not compute. 256 HP at 5400 RPM requires 249 ftlbs. Maybe you mean 7400 RPM?<BR>I would think that with a (7400-4800) 2600 RPM powerband it would do pretty well, but your the one with experience. I guess the Getrag doesn't match a high revving engine very well.<P><B><P>We detuned the motor - milder cams, more restrictive exhaust, smaller TB, and re-curved ECM. It ended up 238hp (still more than a hundred ponies per liter!) with 208lbft of torque. Both the horsepower and torque came on at a lower rpm, and had a flatter curve. The Fiero performed (and actually is still winning races) a lot better than when it had the "potent" 250+ horses.<BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is that a typo? If not, that is <B><I>@#$%^&</B></I> incredible torque! Over 90 ftlbs/litre! The new M3 only has 82.<BR>You gotta share a build sheet with us. What in the world did you put into that engine? Ground up unicorn horn in the oil?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000011||cadero2dmax||12-21-2001||08:20 PM||||Now you know why Greg Duncan (who bought the car from me) is getting FTD with the now fattened up 2000 lb Fiero over Formula Fords and Z06's. BTW, he is still beating the 4.9 powered team Python car, too!!<P>The engine was built by Mantapart. It wasn't a good, positive experience, but bottom line the engine was potent.<P>I no longer have the dyno sheets, and my memory is shot. But the redline was 7400, as you state (the limiter literally shut the engine down, shutting ignition off to 2 cylinders - - ). The powerband when I had the big numbers looked like a tent, it peaked somewhere in the middle, and I recall 5200 or so RPM then dropped off rapidly. The hp and torque curves were similarly shaped, with the hp peaking a little later, but not by much. The torque then dropped off a lot sharper after it peaked than did the horsepower.<P>At $100 an hour, the dyno time added up and helped drive the cost of that engine to around 7 grand in 1998. Think about that, almost 2 grand per cylinder. That is rapidly getting out of the grassroots and into big time. You can almost build a Winston West or Hooters 355ci to about 600hp for $2000 per cylinder!!<P>A long story about me, my Fiero, and the SCCA. Suffice to say that when they started moving my class around, and when a quarter million dollar Porsche was put into my class, I went bananas. I sold the car, quit Fieros altogether for about a year (I'm baaaccck!!) and quit racing. Greg bought the car, and as partial payment built my street 4.9 SE.<P>End of history lesson, and back on topic. The Caddy is built the way it is, to provide a solid torquey engine that will last a long time in a big sedan. To try to open it up would, IMO, lose some of those values that make it such a neat engine for the street in a Fiero. <P>I like mine the way it is - basic stock!!   :-)<P>G<BR><p>[This message has been edited by cadero2dmax (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||1
